  "statement": {
    "representation": "pdf-text-entry",
    "text": "A representation of a group G on a vector space V is called a nil-represen-\ntation if for every v in V and g in G there exists n = n(v, g) such that v(g − 1)n = 0.\nIs it true that in zero characteristic every irreducible nil-representation is trivial? (In\nprime characteristic it is not true.)                                              S. Vovsi",
